## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical OAuth2 debugging skills, understanding of token lifecycle, and real-world troubleshooting abilities that backend engineers face daily.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates identify: 1) Refresh token expired/revoked, 2) Scope mismatch between access and refresh tokens, 3) Client configuration issues. They should mention checking token introspection endpoint, reviewing logs, and testing with Postman/curl.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Debug refresh token failure
async function debugRefreshToken(refreshToken: string) {
  try {
    // 1. Check token introspection
    const introspect = await fetch('/oauth/introspect', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: `token=${refreshToken}`
    });

    // 2. Validate client configuration
    const clientConfig = await getClientConfig();

    // 3. Review token storage
    const storedToken = await getStoredToken(refreshToken);

    return { introspect, clientConfig, storedToken };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Debug failed:', error);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design multi-protocol contract governance, versioning, and cross-protocol mappings in a real product. It tests practical approaches to keep REST, GraphQL, and gRPC synchronized, handle migrations with minimal disruption, and implement tests and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Single source of truth for OpenAPI, SDL, and proto contracts
- Versioned envelopes and per-endpoint migrations
- Deprecation windows and non-breaking changes
- Cross-protocol mappings (REST -> GraphQL -> gRPC)
- Consumer-driven contract tests, validation, and observability (tracing/metrics)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal versioned contract envelope
class ContractEnvelope {
  constructor(version, restOpenAPI, graphqlSDL, grpcProto) {
    this.version = version;
    this.rest = restOpenAPI;
    this.graphql = graphqlSDL;
    this.grpc = grpcProto;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate contract generation and validation across all three protocols?
- How would you handle breaking changes and feature flag migrations without affecting existing clients?
- What testing strategy would you use to ensure cross-protocol semantics stay in sync over time?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design scalable, resilient multi-region systems with per-tenant isolation, hot-tenant mitigation, and cost control—core competencies for distributed systems engineers at senior levels.

## Key Concepts

- Time-based shard design and tenant isolation strategies
- Multi-region replication with quorum-based consistency
- Hot-tenant mitigation and dynamic shard rebalancing
- Data lifecycle management: tiered storage, retention, compaction, and archival

## Code Example

```javascript
function shardKey(tenantId, timestamp) {
  const hourBucket = Math.floor(timestamp / 3600000);
  return `${tenantId}:${hourBucket}`;
}

class TimeSeriesStore {
  constructor(regions, replicationFactor = 3) {
    this.regions = regions;
    this.replicationFactor = replicationFactor;
    this.quorum = Math.floor(replicationFactor / 2) + 1;
  }

  async write(tenantId, timestamp, data) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const writes = replicas.map(region => this.writeToRegion(region, key, data));
    await Promise.all(writes);
    return this.verifyQuorum(writes);
  }

  selectReplicas(shardKey) {
    return this.regions.slice(0, this.replicationFactor);
  }

  async read(tenantId, timestamp) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const reads = replicas.map(region => this.readFromRegion(region, key));
    return this.mergeResults(await Promise.all(reads));
  }
}
```

